WebGL 1 page contexts get a WebGL 1 host context.

The engine was linked for WebGL 2 only, so WebGL 1 page contexts ran on WebGL
2 host contexts: pages lost the WebGL 1 extensions (standard derivatives,
float textures, vertex array objects...) and GLSL ES 1.00 shaders could not
enable them. WebGL 1 page contexts now share a host WebGL 1 context of their
own, alongside the WebGL 2 one (still at most three host contexts per worker
with the engine's). Internal formats WebCore sizes for ES 3 drivers (float,
half-float, depth and sRGB textures) reach the WebGL 1 host unsized.

